INT. ICE HOUSE - BEHIND THE LIVERY - DAY

Sawdust floors. Blocks of river ice stacked to the rafters, steaming in the heat. Cold breath hangs in the air.

OTIS WREN, 60s, leather apron, hands like split kindling, chips at a block with a pick. Slow. Methodical.

The door scrapes open. Light cuts the dark.

IDA MARROW, 30s, dust-caked duster, rifle sling across her chest, steps in. She lets the door close behind her.

He doesn't look up.

                    OTIS
Ice is two bits a pound.

                    IDA
I'm not buying ice.

He keeps chipping. She walks the aisle between the stacks. Runs a glove along the frost.

                    OTIS
Then you're lost.

                    IDA
Your boy Wade. Where is he?

The pick stops. He sets it down. Wipes his hands on the apron, one finger at a time.

                    OTIS
Wade's in Cheyenne.

                    IDA
Wade was in Cheyenne in March.

Behind a far stack, a boot scuffs. She hears it. He hears her hear it.

                    OTIS
Rats.

                    IDA
Big ones.

She slides the rifle off her shoulder. Doesn't raise it. Lets it hang.

WADE WREN, 20s, thin, sunburned, steps out from the ice with a pistol low against his thigh. His eyes are red-rimmed. He hasn't slept.

                    WADE
Pa, go back to work.

                    OTIS
Son, hush.

                    WADE
She's alone.

                    IDA
Am I?

Wade glances at the door. At the dark gaps between the stacks. His grip tightens.

                    WADE
You're bluffing.

                    IDA
Maybe.

Silence. Water drips from the rafters. It ticks on the sawdust.

                    OTIS
What'd he take from you?

                    IDA
My brother's horse. And my brother's life, after.

                    WADE
He drew first.

                    IDA
He was nineteen. He was holding a hat.

Wade flinches. Otis sees it. Something in the old man's shoulders drops a half inch.

                    OTIS
Wade.

                    WADE
He had his hand under his coat.

                    IDA
For the hat.

                    OTIS
(quiet)
Put the pistol down.

                    WADE
No.

A thin figure moves in the doorway, backlit. A shadow first, then a man.

SILAS PRUETT, 50s, tin star on a faded vest, holds a shotgun with both hands. He walks in like a man paying a debt.

                    SILAS
Evening, all.

                    IDA
It's noon.

                    SILAS
Feels later.

He stops by the ice block nearest Otis. Takes in the room. Three people. Two guns. One question.

                    SILAS
Wade, you're under arrest.

                    WADE
For what?

                    SILAS
Murder, son. I've got a warrant in my pocket I've been carrying three weeks.

                    OTIS
Three weeks.

                    SILAS
I'm slow.

                    IDA
You're late.

                    SILAS
Same thing.

Wade's pistol swings between them. Silas. Ida. Silas. His breath comes shallow and fast.

                    WADE
I ain't going to hang.

                    SILAS
Nobody's hanging anybody today.

                    IDA
I'd like to.

                    SILAS
I know you would, ma'am.

Otis takes one step. Puts himself between his son and the rifle. Arms open, palms out.

                    OTIS
Everybody breathe.

Nobody does.

A block of ice shifts overhead. Groans. Cracks.

They all look up.

                    OTIS (CONT'D)
That's the chain.

                    SILAS
Move.

                    WADE
Don't tell me--

                    SILAS (CONT'D)
^
Move!

                    IDA
^
Get out of there!

The chain snaps. A block the size of a wagon bed drops, hits the floor, and bursts. Shards fly. Everyone dives.

Silence. Then coughing. Ice dust drifts like smoke.

Wade scrambles up first, pistol still in hand, ears ringing. He sees Ida on one knee, rifle raised, trained on his chest.

He raises his own.

Both freeze.

                    IDA
Drop it.

                    WADE
You drop it.

Silas drags himself upright behind the shattered block, shotgun levelled at them both. Blood on his forehead. His hands do not shake.

                    SILAS
Nobody's dropping anything.

Otis is on the floor. A shard of ice has pinned his leg. He groans, grips the sawdust.

                    OTIS
Wade.

Wade looks down. His father's face is gray.

                    OTIS (CONT'D)
Look at me. Not her.

Wade looks. His pistol wavers.

Ida's finger moves on the trigger. Silas sees it.

                    SILAS
Ida Marrow. Don't.

                    IDA
Why not?

                    SILAS
Because I'm the one who's going to have to write it up.

A beat. Almost a laugh. It doesn't land.

Wade takes a breath. Another. He lowers the pistol an inch. Two.

Ida doesn't.

                    IDA
Say his name.

                    WADE
What?

                    IDA
My brother. Say it.

Wade's mouth works. Nothing comes.

                    OTIS
(through his teeth)
Say it, boy.

                    WADE
Tom.

                    IDA
Tom what?

                    WADE
Tom Marrow.

She holds the rifle on him another long second. Then the barrel dips.

Wade drops the pistol. It thuds into the sawdust.

Silas moves in fast. Cuffs. Click, click. Wade doesn't resist. He's crying without a sound.

Ida stands over Otis. Lifts the ice shard off his leg with one hard heave. Tosses it aside.

                    OTIS
Why'd you do that?

                    IDA
Somebody should leave this place standing.

She shoulders the rifle. Walks to the door. Light pours around her.

                    SILAS
Ma'am. You'll need to testify.

                    IDA
I know.

She goes. The door scrapes shut.

Drip. Drip. The ice keeps melting.
